    "label": "30",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p31-q30-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In the first enrichment set of exercises of this chapter, the ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "extended Pascal’s Triangle",
              "marks": [
                "italic"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " was introduced to expand expressions of the form ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\frac{1}{(1+x)^n}=(1+x)^{-n}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " by using the ‘negative’ rows of the triangle.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p31-q30-series-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In particular, note the following results.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p31-q30-series",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x}&=1+x+x^2+x^3+x^4+\\cdots\\\\\\frac{1}{(1-x)^2}&=1+2x+3x^2+4x^3+5x^4+\\cdots\\\\\\frac{1}{(1-x)^3}&=1+3x+6x^2+10x^3+15x^4+\\cdots\\\\\\frac{1}{(1-x)^4}&=1+4x+10x^2+20x^3+35x^4+\\cdots\\end{aligned}",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p31-q30-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $$ (x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4=\\frac{x^4}{(1-x)^4}. $$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle (x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4=x^4(1+x+x^2+\\cdots)^4=\\frac{x^4}{(1-x)^4}$.",
          "worked": "Factor $x$ from each bracket and use the supplied geometric series.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4&=x^4(1+x+x^2+\\cdots)^4\\\\(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4&=x^4\\left(\\frac{1}{1-x}\\right)^4\\\\(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4&=\\frac{x^4}{(1-x)^4}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p31-q30-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the coefficient of $x^7$ in the expansion of $$(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$20$.\n\nMultiplying by $x^4$ shifts the $x^3$ coefficient of $\\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4}$ to the $x^7$ coefficient.",
          "worked": "Using part (a) and the supplied expansion,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4&=x^4(1+4x+10x^2+20x^3+35x^4+\\cdots)\\\\(x+x^2+x^3+\\cdots)^4&=x^4+4x^5+10x^6+20x^7+35x^8+\\cdots.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x^7$ is $20$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
